-- title:
--   Theorem 4: local SPCAs with a memory threshold yield a geometrically local Floquet Lindbladian with local classical registers that is a quantum $(q_X+q_Z)$-memory at cycle boundaries
-- statement:
--   Let $(\mathcal C_L)_{L\in\mathbb N}$ be a family of CSS codes on $n(L)$ qubits whose X- and Z-interaction graphs have degree at most $D$ for every $L$. For each $L$ fix a designated logical qubit and readout decoders, with logical-error indicators $b^L_X,b^L_Z$. Let $a^{(X,L)},a^{(Z,L)}$ be update rules that are $R_X$-, $R_Z$-local in the interaction graphs of $C^L_X,C^L_Z$. Suppose there are $p_c>0$ and $q_X,q_Z:\mathbb N\times\mathbb N\to\mathbb R$ such that, for every $0\le p<p_c$, every $L$ and every $m$, the SPCA $\Psi^L_\sigma$ with rule $a^{(\sigma,L)}$ at stochastic-adversarial fault rate $p$, started at $0$, $q_\sigma(m,L)$-remembers $b^L_\sigma$ through round $m$ ($\sigma\in\{X,Z\}$).
--
--   Then there exist $\eta_c>0$, $\Delta>0$ and $R,K\in\mathbb N$, independent of $L$, and for each $L$ a register set with at most $K$ classical registers per site and a register-extended syndrome-based correction Lindbladian $\mathcal L^L_c(t)$ on the data qubits and these registers, such that:
--
--   1. $\mathcal L^L_c$ is periodic with period $\Delta$, $R$-local, and equivariant under every symmetry of the code pair and both rules (translation invariance);
--   2. for every $L$, every $\eta<\eta_c$, every Pauli noise $\mathcal L^{\mathrm{ext}}_{\mathrm{noise}}(t)$ of rate $\eta$ on the data qubits and the registers, with propagator $W$ of $\mathcal L^L_c(t)+\mathcal L^{\mathrm{ext}}_{\mathrm{noise}}(t)$, and every $m$,
--   $$F_{\min}\Big[\rho\mapsto\operatorname{Tr}_{\mathrm{reg}}W(r\Delta)\big(\rho\otimes|0\rangle\langle0|_{\mathrm{reg}}\big)\Big]\ \ge\ 1-\big(q_X(m,L)+q_Z(m,L)\big)\qquad\text{for every }0\le r\le m .$$
--
--   Thus any pair of local synchronous classical memories with a nonzero fault threshold can be implemented as an autonomous, geometrically local, periodically driven quantum memory whose fidelity at cycle boundaries inherits the classical retention bounds.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - encoding: n-qubit CSS code with a designated logical qubit (Definitions 2.1, 2.8–2.9) → n = n'+1 qubits, the designated qubit is qubit 0, and the conjugate-pair facts H_Z ℓ_X = 0, H_X ℓ_Z = 0, ℓ_X·ℓ_Z = 1 are explicit (the base mission's LogicalQubit); reason: the encoding unitary is arbitrary, so naming qubit 0 loses no generality; same instances
--   - reading: a Lindbladian 'acting on the code qubits together with local classical registers' → a register-extended syndrome-based Lindbladian (Definition 7.1) on the data and a sector-labelled register set with at most K registers per site, K independent of L; reason: Definition 7.1 is the paper's formal class for classical registers (registers read only through Z_j), proved of the construction in Claim 7.8; locality of the registers is the per-site bound of Lemma 7.11
--   - reading: geometrically local → R-local in the sense of Definition 2.23 in the CSS interaction graph, registers counted at their site, R independent of L; reason: the paragraph after Definition 7.6 (p. 56) states this form of locality for L_PCA
--   - reading: periodic Lindbladian with period Δ → the Hamiltonian and every syndrome function of the representation satisfy X(t + Δ) = X(t) for t ≥ 0; reason: the schedule (193) is periodic in this sense
--   - reading: translationally invariant whenever the code pair and both SPCAs are → for every permutation of sites and checks preserving H_X, H_Z and commuting with both rules on valid syndromes, there is a register permutation moving each register with its site and keeping its sector, and L_c commutes with the combined conjugation; reason: the code family carries no lattice structure; translations are the special case of a translation-invariant family
--   - generalization: local Pauli noise with constant rates 0 ≤ γ_{P,i}, γ^reg_{P,j} ≤ η (Definition 7.7, eq. (194)) → measurable time-dependent rates with values in [0, η] for t ≥ 0; reason: Remark 2.26 states that measurable, locally bounded time-dependent rates are covered verbatim and that Section 7 uses the rates only through the bound η; constant rates are the special case
--   - encoding: L_PCA(t) := L_c(t) + L^aug_noise (eq. (195)) → the witness is the correction part L_c; the evolution is generated by L_c(t) + L^ext_noise(t) with the propagator in integrated form (Reading R1); reason: same dynamics; the propagator of a measurable, locally bounded generator exists and is unique
--   - encoding: F_min^ext at the cycle boundaries t_r = rΔ, registers prepared in |0⟩ and traced out (Corollary 7.3) → the base Fmin of ρ ↦ Tr_reg W(rΔ)(ρ ⊗ |0⟩⟨0|_reg), for every r ≤ m; reason: the effective logical channel of Corollary 7.3; the infimum over the finite set of cycle boundaries is the pointwise bound
--   - encoding: codes with bounded-degree interaction graphs and linear size L; Ψ_σ at fault rate p < p_c → a family indexed by L : ℕ with a uniform degree bound D on the interaction graphs of C_X^L and C_Z^L; fault rates 0 ≤ p < p_c; reason: L is only the family index; a fault rate is a probability
--   - encoding: a run of an SPCA (Definition 2.40) → a process on an arbitrary probability space with the update holding off F_r almost surely, F_r of product law, and F_r independent of (e_0, …, e_r, F_0, …, F_{r−1}) by factorization on atoms; rule locality required between valid syndromes; reason: same notion: all variables are finite-valued and a_i is defined on im H
-- source:
--   Drechsler, Oreg, Aharonov, Autonomous Quantum Memories from Classical Stochastic Processes, arXiv:2610.05589 (PDF of 2026-11-03), Theorem 4, p. 31 (PDF p. 33); proof §7, pp. 50–61 (PDF pp. 52–63); Definitions 7.1, 7.4–7.7, pp. 52–56 (PDF pp. 54–58)

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_AutoQMem_Lindblad_Supplement_IsQuantumMemoryThrough
import Definitions.Def_AutoQMem_Lindblad_Supplement_SPCA
import Definitions.Def_AutoQMem_Lindblad_Supplement_extDynamics
import Definitions.Def_AutoQMem_Lindblad_Supplement_extLocality
